messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2026,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2028,
      "event": "AI systems become ordinary tools in professional work.",
      "detail": "Capability keeps rising and adoption runs ahead of regulation. Gains concentrate in organizations that already have capital, data, and technical staff, and reliability remains uneven."
    },
    {
      "year": 2036,
      "event": "Global temperature stays above 1.5°C relative to the late nineteenth century.",
      "detail": "Past emissions and continued fossil use make a sustained overshoot likely. Heat, floods, and crop losses become more frequent, with the worst harm in poorer and hotter regions."
    },
    {
      "year": 2045,
      "event": "Automation removes a large share of routine cognitive and manual jobs.",
      "detail": "New work appears, but not fast enough or in the same places. States try retraining, work programs, and cash transfers, and none of these fully resolves the political conflict."
    },
    {
      "year": 2055,
      "event": "World population peaks and begins a slow decline.",
      "detail": "The peak is most likely between about 9 and 11 billion. Fertility is already low across most of East Asia and Europe, while parts of Africa are still growing."
    },
    {
      "year": 2068,
      "event": "A serious new pandemic kills millions before it is contained.",
      "detail": "Better vaccines and surveillance make a 1918-scale disaster less likely, not unlikely. Travel, animal reservoirs, and laboratory work keep large outbreaks on the table."
    },
    {
      "year": 2080,
      "event": "Chronic heat and water stress repeatedly shock food markets.",
      "detail": "Global trade and farm technology prevent a permanent return to premodern famine in most years. Local harvest failures, migration, and price spikes still become a normal part of politics."
    },
    {
      "year": 2095,
      "event": "Almost everyone alive in 2026 is dead, and the demographic map has shifted.",
      "detail": "Africa holds a much larger share of world population. East Asia and Europe are older and smaller, and several of today's pension and migration bargains have broken."
    },
    {
      "year": 2115,
      "event": "Warming settles near 2–3°C, with lasting ecological loss.",
      "detail": "Emissions peak and fall, but too late to avoid major damage. Many coral systems, glaciers, and wild species are gone or reduced to remnants, while average material living standards are still higher than in 2026."
    },
    {
      "year": 2150,
      "event": "Low-carbon electricity is normal, and a small Moon presence is continuous.",
      "detail": "Solar, wind, storage, and fission do most of the work; fusion is minor or absent. Mars has episodic crews, not a city, and space settlement is not a backup for Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Medicine lengthens healthy life by years but does not defeat aging.",
      "detail": "Cancer, heart disease, and dementia are more manageable for people with access. Living past about 120 remains rare, and unequal access to care is still a major political fact."
    },
    {
      "year": 2280,
      "event": "Several large states of our century no longer exist inside their 2026 borders.",
      "detail": "The causes are aging, separatism, war, and negotiated breakup rather than one world state. Trade and some technical standards outlive the governments that wrote them."
    },
    {
      "year": 2350,
      "event": "Sea level is still rising long after emissions have peaked.",
      "detail": "Ice sheets respond over centuries. Some districts defended in the late twenty-first century have been abandoned, and coastal maps no longer match those of 2026."
    },
    {
      "year": 2450,
      "event": "A limited strategic-weapon war has occurred without ending industrial civilization.",
      "detail": "If large arsenals persist for centuries, permanent non-use becomes less plausible than one or more limited uses. A full exchange that collapses every industrial region is still not the median path."
    },
    {
      "year": 2600,
      "event": "Heritable genetic edits and implants are common, and some cultures refuse them.",
      "detail": "Human bodies and cognition vary more by design than they do today. The species has not cleanly divided, but descendant populations are already drifting apart."
    },
    {
      "year": 2800,
      "event": "No human colony exists beyond the Solar System, and no alien civilization has made contact.",
      "detail": "Robotic probes have left the system. Interstellar settlement remains a multi-century project, and the galaxy still looks silent to ordinary observers."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "At least one regional industrial civilization has collapsed and later been partly rebuilt.",
      "detail": "Knowledge is not lost everywhere at once. Political and religious life has no institutional continuity with the states and faiths of the twenty-first century."
    },
    {
      "year": 3300,
      "event": "Some human-descended lineages are different enough that speciation is a real question.",
      "detail": "Isolation, selection, and deliberate modification all contribute. Other groups remain closer to ancestral bodies through doctrine, poverty, or choice."
    },
    {
      "year": 3600,
      "event": "Records of our era survive only as damaged and disputed archives.",
      "detail": "What later people believe about the twenty-first century is a mixture of data, forgery, and myth. Continuous universities, states, and churches from our time do not exist."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Large-scale war still happens with weapons unrecognizable to us.",
      "detail": "Treaties and surveillance sometimes restrain the worst tools and sometimes fail. Permanent peace is not the likely outcome of another two millennia."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "No language commonly spoken in 2026 remains a living mother tongue.",
      "detail": "English, Mandarin, Spanish, Arabic, and the others persist, if at all, as scholarly or ritual languages. Ordinary speech has passed through forms we could not understand."
    },
    {
      "year": 5000,
      "event": "Scientific knowledge persists in places after repeated loss and reinvention.",
      "detail": "Smooth progress from today's institutions is very unlikely. So is permanent global forgetting of agriculture, germs, and basic mechanics."
    },
    {
      "year": 5500,
      "event": "Most human descendants still live on Earth, not in a settled galaxy.",
      "detail": "Off-world habitats are few, fragile, or already abandoned. A self-sustaining expansion to other stars is not the median path by this date."
    },
    {
      "year": 6000,
      "event": "The industrial era's ecological mark is a geological fact rather than a remembered crisis.",
      "detail": "Sediments, extinctions, and altered coastlines still record our time. Living people do not experience that record as our climate politics."
    },
    {
      "year": 6500,
      "event": "Non-biological intelligence is a major historical actor wherever technical society continues.",
      "detail": "I cannot say whether those systems are tools, partners, or successors. The durable change is that high-level cognition is no longer only biological."
    },
    {
      "year": 7000,
      "event": "Unmodified ancestral human bodies are a minority condition.",
      "detail": "Some communities preserve them on purpose. Most intelligent descendants of humanity are altered organisms, artificial systems, or hybrids."
    },
    {
      "year": 7500,
      "event": "Civilization has not become a stable solar-system-scale energy empire.",
      "detail": "Energy use can be very large by our standards and still far below a Dyson swarm. Coordination failures and weak incentives cap expansion."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Religions and political ideas of 2026 survive only as transformed fragments.",
      "detail": "Names may persist after the original doctrines are gone. Concern for distant descendants is practiced by some cultures and ignored by others."
    },
    {
      "year": 8500,
      "event": "Natural planetary hazards have not, on the median path, sterilized Earth.",
      "detail": "Impacts, volcanoes, and solar extremes can cause terrible regional damage. They are less likely than internal technological failure to remove every human-descended lineage."
    },
    {
      "year": 9200,
      "event": "The surviving danger is mainly war, engineered disease, unmanaged machines, or slow decline.",
      "detail": "These risks recur rather than disappearing after one close call. Which of them, if any, has already cut off part of the lineage cannot be identified from here."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Some human-originated intelligence still exists, but not as Homo sapiens in the 2026 sense.",
      "detail": "That is my low-confidence median, not a fact. Extinction of every biological and artificial continuation before this year is plausible enough that survival should not be treated as assured."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "uncertain",
  "summary": "The median path is not a clean utopia or a dated extinction. It is a long, uneven technological civilization: climate damage, pandemics, automation, and political breakage in the next centuries, then slow adaptation, augmentation, and cultural replacement. By 10000, something descended from us or built by us may remain, but whether it would still be humanity is an open question, and total loss before then is plausible."
}
